
<% content_for :title, "Urgent According to Who? | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="urgency-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="urgency-hero">

    <picture class="urgency-background" aria-hidden="true">
      <source
        media="(min-width: 768px)"
        srcset="<%= asset_path('toolbox_urgent_according_to_who_desktop.webp') %>"
      >
      <%= image_tag "toolbox_urgent_according_to_who_mobile.webp",
          alt: "",
          loading: "eager" %>
    </picture>

    <div class="urgency-hero-content">

      <p class="urgency-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / TOOLBOX / URGENT ACCORDING TO WHO?
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>URGENT</span>
        <span>ACCORDING</span>
        <span>TO WHO?</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="urgency-intro">
        JUST BECAUSE IT FEELS URGENT
        DOESN'T MEAN IT NEEDS
        TO HAPPEN RIGHT NOW.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / THE URGENCY -->
  <section class="urgency-section urgency-pink">
    <div class="urgency-inner">

      <p class="urgency-number">01 / THE URGENCY</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT CAN'T<br>
        FUCKING WAIT?
      </h2>

      <div class="urgency-copy">

        <p>
          You have an idea, a problem,
          or something you want,
          and suddenly it needs
          to happen immediately.
        </p>

        <p>
          Maybe it's a project,
          a purchase, a conversation,
          or a change you've
          decided to make.
        </p>

        <p>
          The more you think
          about it, the more
          important it feels.
          Waiting becomes frustrating,
          and you start treating
          your own excitement
          or discomfort like
          an actual deadline.
        </p>

        <p>
          But wanting something
          to happen isn't the
          same as needing
          it to happen.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can have a
          worthwhile idea without
          having to act on
          it immediately.
        </p>

        <p>
          The feeling of urgency
          is worth noticing,
          but it isn't proof
          that the situation
          requires immediate action.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="urgency-callout">
        <p>
          WHAT EXACTLY FEELS URGENT?
          WHAT HAPPENS IF YOU WAIT?
          IS THERE AN ACTUAL PROBLEM,
          OR DO YOU JUST WANT
          SOMETHING TO HAPPEN NOW?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / CHECK THE DEADLINE -->
  <section class="urgency-section urgency-black">
    <div class="urgency-inner">

      <p class="urgency-number">02 / CHECK THE DEADLINE</p>

      <h2>
        WHERE'S THE<br>
        ACTUAL DEADLINE?
      </h2>

      <div class="urgency-copy">

        <p>
          Some things genuinely
          require immediate attention.
          Medical emergencies, important
          appointments, and obligations
          with real deadlines
          shouldn't be ignored.
        </p>

        <p>
          But not everything
          demanding your attention
          belongs in that category.
        </p>

        <p>
          Ask yourself who established
          the deadline. Is there a
          specific date, consequence,
          or person depending
          on you to act?
        </p>

        <p>
          Or have you decided
          something needs to
          happen today because
          you're excited, anxious,
          irritated, or uncomfortable
          with waiting?
        </p>

        <p>
          You can take something
          seriously without
          treating it like
          an emergency.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="urgency-grid">

        <div class="urgency-box">
          <h3>WHAT NEEDS ACTION NOW?</h3>

          <p>
            Immediate safety concerns
            or serious medical symptoms.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bills, appointments,
            and responsibilities
            with genuine deadlines.
          </p>

          <p>
            Situations where delaying
            would create a specific consequence.
          </p>

          <p>
            Identify the necessary
            action and take care
            of that part.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="urgency-box">
          <h3>WHAT JUST FEELS URGENT?</h3>

          <p>
            A new project you
            suddenly want to start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Something you want
            to buy before you've
            considered the cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            A message you're
            impatient to send
            or receive.
          </p>

          <p>
            A problem you want
            resolved because
            uncertainty is uncomfortable.
          </p>

          <p>
            If there's no real
            deadline, you can
            choose when to act.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  

  <!-- 03 / PARK THE PLAN -->
  <section class="urgency-section urgency-purple">
    <div class="urgency-inner">

      <p class="urgency-number">04 / PARK THE PLAN</p>

      <h2>
        WRITE IT DOWN.<br>
        LET IT SIT.
      </h2>

      <div class="urgency-copy">

        <p>
          You don't have to
          abandon an idea
          simply because it
          doesn't need to
          happen immediately.
        </p>

        <p>
          Write it down.
          Save a reference image.
          Make a short plan.
          Record what interests
          you about it.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can preserve
          an idea without
          committing money,
          time, or resources
          to it right now.
        </p>

        <p>
          Give yourself a
          reasonable time
          to revisit it.
          Waiting allows you
          to see whether
          the idea still
          makes sense after
          the initial urgency
          has settled.
        </p>

      

      </div>

      <div class="urgency-grid">

        <div class="urgency-box">
          <h3>THE IDEA.</h3>

          <p>
            What exactly
            do you want
            to accomplish?
          </p>

          <p>
            Why does it
            interest you?
          </p>

          <p>
            Write down the
            concept without
            turning it into
            an immediate project.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can decide
            whether to pursue
            it later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="urgency-box">
          <h3>THE COST &amp; COMMITMENT.</h3>

          <p>
            How much money,
            time, energy,
            or preparation
            will this require?
          </p>

          <p>
            Do you already
            have the materials
            or resources?
          </p>

          <p>
            What existing
            responsibilities
            might it affect?
          </p>

          <p>
            Consider the full
            commitment rather
            than only the
            exciting beginning.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="urgency-box">
          <h3>WHAT HAPPENS IF I WAIT?</h3>

          <p>
            Will anything
            genuinely be lost
            by delaying?
          </p>

          <p>
            Is there a real
            deadline, or does
            waiting simply
            feel uncomfortable?
          </p>

          <p>
            Could you use
            the time to
            gather information
            or reconsider
            the plan?
          </p>

          <p>
            A worthwhile idea
            can still be
            worthwhile tomorrow.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="urgency-box">
          <h3>WHEN I'LL REVISIT IT.</h3>

          <p>
            Choose a reasonable
            time to return
            to the decision.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tomorrow might work
            for a small,
            reversible choice.
            Larger commitments
            may deserve
            more time.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your mood
            is elevated,
            wait for greater
            stability and
            involve appropriate
            support.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't have
            to resolve
            everything today.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / ANOTHER PERSPECTIVE -->
  <section class="urgency-section urgency-black">
    <div class="urgency-inner">

      <p class="urgency-number">05 / ANOTHER PERSPECTIVE</p>

      <h2>
        RUN IT<br>
        BY SOMEONE.
      </h2>

      <div class="urgency-copy">

        <p>
          When you're excited
          about something,
          you can become
          convinced that
          your reasoning
          is completely sound.
        </p>

        <p>
          You know why
          the idea makes sense,
          why the purchase
          is worthwhile,
          or why the plan
          needs to happen.
        </p>

        <p>
          But feeling certain
          doesn't mean you've
          considered everything.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes another person
          can recognize practical
          concerns you've overlooked
          or notice that your
          behavior has changed.
        </p>

        <p>
          Ask someone you trust
          whether the timeline
          seems reasonable.
          You don't need them
          to make the decision
          for you.
        </p>


        <p>
          Some situations require
          more than simply
          waiting a day
          before acting.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="urgency-callout">
        <p>
          "I HAVE AN IDEA THAT FEELS
          REALLY IMPORTANT RIGHT NOW.
          CAN I RUN IT BY YOU?
          I WANT TO KNOW WHETHER
          THE TIMELINE MAKES SENSE
          OR IF I'M RUSHING INTO IT."
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / YOUR NEXT MOVE -->
  <section class="urgency-section urgency-pink">
    <div class="urgency-inner">

      <p class="urgency-number">06 / YOUR NEXT MOVE</p>

      <h2>
        CHOOSE THE<br>
        NEXT STEP.<br>
        NOT ALL OF THEM.
      </h2>

      <div class="urgency-copy">

        <p>
          Once you've checked
          the deadline, your
          current pace, and
          the practical details,
          decide what actually
          needs to happen next.
        </p>

        <p>
          If there's a genuine
          deadline, take the
          necessary action.
          You don't have to
          turn one responsibility
          into five additional tasks.
        </p>

        <p>
          If there's no deadline,
          you can schedule the
          next step, collect
          more information,
          or leave the plan
          alone for now.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can be excited
          about a project
          without starting it
          immediately. You can
          want an answer without
          demanding one today.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can recognize
          an opportunity without
          assuming it will
          disappear if you
          don't act right away.
        </p>


        <p>
          You don't need to
          suppress your enthusiasm
          or ignore a worthwhile
          idea. You need to
          separate its importance
          from the urgency
          you're assigning to it.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            SOMETHING CAN MATTER
            WITHOUT NEEDING
            TO HAPPEN TODAY.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="urgency-callout">
        <p>
          WHO SET THE DEADLINE?
          WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENS
          IF YOU WAIT?
          WHAT'S ONE REASONABLE
          NEXT STEP — AND WHAT
          CAN YOU LEAVE ALONE?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

</main>
